title: Investigate how beads is actually used in ../idol
effort: M
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T06:54:13Z
updated: 2026-09-17T06:54:13Z
handoff:

idol is the most mature project on beads and clockwork-city, so it is the
only evidence available about which bd concepts are load-bearing in
practice rather than in bd's feature list. 6 cannot be planned honestly
without it.

What is there, from a shallow look:

  .beads/issues.jsonl        342 issues
  .beads/interactions.jsonl  455 interactions
  .beads/embeddeddolt/       an embedded Dolt database
  .beads/                    94M total

The 94M is worth stating plainly, since pidly's premise is that the
repository is the interface: the whole of pidly's own tracker is 37 tickets
of plain text, and the comparison belongs in the design rather than in a
sales pitch.

An issue carries: _type, assignee, close_reason, closed_at, comment_count,
created_at, created_by, dependency_count, dependent_count, description, id,
issue_type, owner, priority, started_at, status, title, updated_at.

Questions to answer from the data, not from opinion:

- Which fields are actually populated across the 342, and how often. A field
  every issue leaves at its default is not a requirement pidly has to meet.
- assignee, owner and created_by are three identities. Are they ever
  different from each other? pidly has one, and it means "who holds it now".
- status: how many distinct values are used, and do any of them mean
  something other than open or closed? pidly has two, by location.
- priority: is it used, and is it used to sort, or set once and ignored?
  That is the trigger 34 is waiting on, and this is the place to test it.
- issue_type: what values, and would they be a project field, a parent
  ticket, or nothing.
- dependency_count and dependent_count are derived. Is the graph shallow
  enough that pidly's needs: covers it, and are there relation types beyond
  blocking — bd has relates-to, duplicates, supersedes, discovered-from,
  which the design rules out on principle 3.
- close_reason and the 455 interactions: what is in them, and where would
  that land in pidly — the body, handoff, or nowhere. This is the one that
  could argue for something pidly does not have.
- Are issues ever reopened, and are they ever re-parented.

Then: what a migration would produce. 342 tickets is small enough to convert
in a script — id to number, title to title, status to location, assignee to
claim, dependencies to needs, description plus comments to the body — and
the interesting part is what has no home, not what maps cleanly.

Done when the answers are written into this ticket's body, 6 can be planned
against them, and anything that argues for a change in pidly is its own
ticket rather than a paragraph here.
